---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import QaMock from '../components/QaMock.astro';
import '../styles/case-study.css';
---
<Base
  title="Migration QA Platform · Rodion Ostrovskii"
  description="Case study: an internal QA platform that checks Shopify migration files before import. Used by 9 migration specialists, cuts QA from 4 hours to 1 per migration."
  ogTitle="Shopify Migration QA Platform · case study"
  ogDescription="Will Shopify accept this file? A tool that answers before the client import."
  active="projects"
>
  <main>
    <section class="section page-head">
      <p class="label">(case study) <b>01</b></p>
      <h1 class="page-title page-title--long">Shopify Migration QA Platform</h1>

      <!-- Swap this illustration for a real screenshot taken on SAMPLE data only, once your manager approves -->
      <figure class="case-cover">
        <div aria-hidden="true"><QaMock large /></div>
        <figcaption class="caption">Illustration with sample data. No client data is shown.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="lede">Will Shopify accept this file? I designed and built an internal tool that answers before anything touches a client store.</p>

      <dl class="facts">
        <div class="fact"><dt>Role</dt><dd>Sole engineer</dd></div>
        <div class="fact"><dt>Users</dt><dd>9 migration specialists</dd></div>
        <div class="fact"><dt>Scale</dt><dd>50k+ customers, 200k+ SKUs</dd></div>
        <div class="fact"><dt>Where</dt><dd>Helios Technology, 2026</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="label label--heading">The problem</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Migration specialists move merchants' customers and products into Shopify from CSV exports. Shopify rejects rows for reasons that aren't obvious from the file: a variant that duplicates another, a blank option value, an email Shopify won't accept. The team often found out after importing, then had to clean up the store and try again.</p>
        <p>Checking a file by hand took about four hours per migration.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="label label--heading">What I built</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>A web app with two sections, customers and products, sharing one server, one client and one PostgreSQL database.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><b>Upload</b>Drop in the CSV. Customer files get a column-mapping step; product files are already in Shopify's format.</li>
        <li><b>Pre-check</b>Rules flag what Shopify will reject, judged on the exact rows the import will send.</li>
        <li><b>Test import</b>Import into one or more test stores in parallel through Shopify's bulk API.</li>
        <li><b>Report</b>Every rejection traced to its source row and grouped by field and error code, with an Excel export.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="label label--heading">Engineering decisions</h2>
      <ul class="bullets">
        <li><strong>Only report what Shopify would reject.</strong> Each pre-check rule is checked against a real test-store result. A rule that fires on data Shopify accepts is noise, so it doesn't ship.</li>
        <li><strong>Judge what gets sent, not the raw file.</strong> The import transforms the data (merging duplicates, tagging, moving notes). The rules run on that transformed data, so the pre-check and the import always agree.</li>
        <li><strong>Built for big files.</strong> Uploads stream to disk instead of loading into memory, and imports use Shopify's bulk operations, so 200k+ SKU catalogs work without a bigger server.</li>
        <li><strong>Merchant data is treated as sensitive.</strong> Raw CSVs are deleted once processed, and a sweep removes anything a crash leaves behind.</li>
        <li><strong>Tested and shipped through CI.</strong> Unit and integration tests (Vitest) run on every pull request in GitHub Actions.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="label label--heading">Results</h2>
      <div class="results">
        <div class="result"><b>4h → 1h</b><span>QA and prep time per migration (75% less)</span></div>
        <div class="result"><b>9 of 9</b><span>migration specialists on the team use it</span></div>
        <div class="result"><b>200k+</b><span>SKUs per file, 50k+ customer records</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="label label--heading">Stack</h2>
      <ul class="tags stack">
        <li>TypeScript</li><li>Node.js</li><li>Express</li><li>React</li><li>Vite</li><li>PostgreSQL</li><li>Prisma</li><li>Shopify Admin GraphQL</li><li>Vitest</li><li>GitHub Actions</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">This is an internal tool at Helios Technology Solutions, so the source code isn't public. I'm happy to walk through the architecture in an interview.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="next-link"><a href="/rewards-platform">Next project: Rewards Platform →</a></p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
